1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,925
It's the first Monday of the month,

2
00:00:01,962 --> 00:00:03,913
and like every first Monday,

3
00:00:03,913 --> 00:00:05,450
Linda, Sarah's mom,

4
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,712
is standing in line at the bank with her

5
00:00:07,712 --> 00:00:08,737
little paper ticket,

6
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:11,275
ready to wait two hours to pay a bill

7
00:00:11,275 --> 00:00:13,012
that the app on her phone would pay in

8
00:00:13,012 --> 00:00:15,550
two minutes. Today Sarah came with her,

9
00:00:15,588 --> 00:00:17,450
and she tries one more time.

10
00:00:17,866 --> 00:00:22,007
"Mom, look, it's three taps and you're done." And

11
00:00:22,007 --> 00:00:23,807
Linda doesn't even look at the phone.

12
00:00:24,238 --> 00:00:25,563
"Apps steal your money.

13
00:00:26,238 --> 00:00:30,130
This line has never robbed me." Sarah has heard

14
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:31,730
that sentence a hundred times,

15
00:00:31,893 --> 00:00:34,018
so she just sighs and settles in for the

16
00:00:34,018 --> 00:00:35,006
next two hours.

17
00:00:35,168 --> 00:00:36,193
So tell me,

18
00:00:36,293 --> 00:00:37,668
do you know someone like this?

19
00:00:37,830 --> 00:00:39,893
A mom who won't touch the banking app,

20
00:00:40,068 --> 00:00:42,380
a dad who still prints every email,

21
00:00:42,493 --> 00:00:45,268
or a grandpa who refuses to video call because

22
00:00:45,268 --> 00:00:46,856
the phone was made for talking?

23
00:00:47,018 --> 00:00:49,580
Well, that person isn't just stubborn.

24
00:00:49,743 --> 00:00:51,756
They're listening to a very old voice,

25
00:00:51,756 --> 00:00:53,793
and today we're going to give it a face.

26
00:00:54,426 --> 00:00:55,814
To understand Linda,

27
00:00:56,038 --> 00:00:58,226
you first need to understand her notebook.

28
00:00:58,388 --> 00:00:59,689
For forty years,

29
00:00:59,851 --> 00:01:01,714
she has written down every bill,

30
00:01:01,864 --> 00:01:04,826
every payment and every coin in the same kind

31
00:01:04,826 --> 00:01:05,651
of blue notebook,

32
00:01:05,814 --> 00:01:07,088
and she has never,

33
00:01:07,088 --> 00:01:09,201
not once, paid anything late.

34
00:01:09,364 --> 00:01:12,276
She raised Sarah on a tight budget and saved

35
00:01:12,276 --> 00:01:13,889
for her university with a pen,

36
00:01:14,088 --> 00:01:16,088
a notebook and that bank line.

37
00:01:16,251 --> 00:01:17,389
So for Linda,

38
00:01:17,389 --> 00:01:19,664
doing things her way isn't just a habit.

39
00:01:19,826 --> 00:01:22,338
It's the proof that she's always been able to

40
00:01:22,338 --> 00:01:23,414
manage on her own.

41
00:01:23,576 --> 00:01:25,514
And she isn't a difficult woman,

42
00:01:25,514 --> 00:01:28,864
either. She's the mom who calls every Sunday and

43
00:01:28,864 --> 00:01:31,101
still sends Sarah home with food for the whole

44
00:01:31,101 --> 00:01:34,358
week. And it's not that Sarah hasn't tried.

45
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:36,795
She has installed the app on her mom's phone

46
00:01:36,795 --> 00:01:39,133
three times, written the steps on a piece of

47
00:01:39,133 --> 00:01:41,908
paper and even offered to sit next to her

48
00:01:41,908 --> 00:01:44,033
every month. But every time,

49
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:45,595
Linda has a new reason.

50
00:01:45,758 --> 00:01:48,395
Her eyes, the buttons are too tiny,

51
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:50,370
the hackers she saw on the news.

52
00:01:50,533 --> 00:01:52,583
And when Sarah answers one reason,

53
00:01:52,583 --> 00:01:53,870
another one shows up.

54
00:01:54,302 --> 00:01:55,415
"Maybe next year,

55
00:01:55,415 --> 00:01:58,796
when I have more time." None of those reasons

56
00:01:58,796 --> 00:02:00,196
is crazy, of course,

57
00:02:00,196 --> 00:02:02,646
and that's exactly what makes it so hard to

58
00:02:02,646 --> 00:02:03,358
argue with her.

59
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:05,770
Then, one Thursday,

60
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:06,920
a letter arrives.

61
00:02:07,083 --> 00:02:08,508
Linda's bank branch,

62
00:02:08,595 --> 00:02:10,820
the one she's been going to for twenty years,

63
00:02:10,983 --> 00:02:12,495
is closing next month,

64
00:02:12,558 --> 00:02:14,895
and the nearest one is across the city,

65
00:02:15,182 --> 00:02:16,470
two buses away.

66
00:02:16,632 --> 00:02:19,058
Linda drops the letter on the kitchen table and

67
00:02:19,058 --> 00:02:20,632
stares at it for a long time.

68
00:02:21,064 --> 00:02:25,016
"Well, then I'll take the two buses." And Sarah

69
00:02:25,016 --> 00:02:27,928
realizes her mom would rather spend four hours on

70
00:02:27,928 --> 00:02:29,378
buses than open an app.

71
00:02:29,541 --> 00:02:32,828
So, what's going on inside people like this?

72
00:02:33,444 --> 00:02:34,819
Let's give it a face.

73
00:02:34,981 --> 00:02:36,006
Meet The Anchor,

74
00:02:36,006 --> 00:02:36,831
the voice that,

75
00:02:36,831 --> 00:02:38,519
whenever something new shows up,

76
00:02:38,769 --> 00:02:41,344
grabs on to the way things are and refuses

77
00:02:41,344 --> 00:02:42,044
to let go.

78
00:02:42,206 --> 00:02:44,331
And here's what makes it so tricky.

79
00:02:44,494 --> 00:02:46,444
The Anchor doesn't sound like fear.

80
00:02:46,606 --> 00:02:48,356
It sounds like common sense,

81
00:02:48,356 --> 00:02:49,931
like experience talking.

82
00:02:50,346 --> 00:02:51,871
"If it has always worked,

83
00:02:51,958 --> 00:02:55,676
why change it?" The catch is that it never

84
00:02:55,676 --> 00:02:57,826
compares the old and the new fairly.

85
00:02:57,988 --> 00:03:00,388
The problems of the old way are familiar,

86
00:03:00,388 --> 00:03:01,688
so they become invisible,

87
00:03:01,764 --> 00:03:03,738
like those two hours in line.

88
00:03:03,901 --> 00:03:06,713
Meanwhile every problem of the new way is fresh,

89
00:03:06,713 --> 00:03:07,864
so it looks huge:

90
00:03:07,988 --> 00:03:10,226
the passwords, the tiny buttons,

91
00:03:10,226 --> 00:03:14,340
the scams. In 1988 two economists,

92
00:03:14,466 --> 00:03:17,253
William Samuelson and Richard Zeckhauser,

93
00:03:17,503 --> 00:03:20,378
looked at how employees at Harvard chose their health

94
00:03:20,378 --> 00:03:23,216
insurance. When new and better plans appeared,

95
00:03:23,353 --> 00:03:24,953
the new hires picked them,

96
00:03:25,016 --> 00:03:26,790
but most of the people who had been there

97
00:03:26,790 --> 00:03:29,665
for years simply kept the plan they already had.

98
00:03:29,828 --> 00:03:32,053
They called it the status quo bias.

99
00:03:32,216 --> 00:03:34,778
So yes, The Anchor visits all of us a

100
00:03:34,778 --> 00:03:37,578
little. But in some people it practically moves in,

101
00:03:37,766 --> 00:03:40,703
especially in people who spent decades doing things one

102
00:03:40,703 --> 00:03:42,740
way, and who feel they have the most to

103
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:44,353
lose if that way disappears.

104
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:46,830
So how do you spot The Anchor in someone

105
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:49,930
you love? Four signs show up again and again.

106
00:03:50,092 --> 00:03:52,617
They defend the old way with the word always.

107
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,292
They can find ten problems in the new option

108
00:03:55,292 --> 00:03:56,480
and none in the old one.

109
00:03:56,642 --> 00:03:57,830
They say I'll try,

110
00:03:57,830 --> 00:03:59,117
just to calm you down,

111
00:03:59,117 --> 00:04:00,530
and then nothing changes.

112
00:04:00,692 --> 00:04:02,505
And their favorite answer is later:

113
00:04:02,755 --> 00:04:03,792
maybe next year,

114
00:04:03,930 --> 00:04:05,367
maybe when I have more time,

115
00:04:05,405 --> 00:04:07,955
and somehow the right moment never comes.

116
00:04:08,118 --> 00:04:10,517
If two or more of those just reminded you

117
00:04:10,517 --> 00:04:11,155
of your mom,

118
00:04:11,255 --> 00:04:12,830
your dad or your grandpa,

119
00:04:12,930 --> 00:04:14,080
you're not imagining it.

120
00:04:14,242 --> 00:04:16,842
You've been living next to a very heavy anchor.

121
00:04:17,466 --> 00:04:19,216
And that anchor has a cost.

122
00:04:19,378 --> 00:04:22,403
Linda now spends four hours a month on buses,

123
00:04:22,403 --> 00:04:24,166
standing, with her bad knee.

124
00:04:24,328 --> 00:04:27,766
Sarah worries every time her mom carries cash across

125
00:04:27,766 --> 00:04:30,554
the city. And they argue about it so often

126
00:04:30,554 --> 00:04:33,341
that the topic sits at every Sunday lunch like

127
00:04:33,341 --> 00:04:34,566
an extra guest.

128
00:04:34,728 --> 00:04:36,641
That's the hidden price of The Anchor:

129
00:04:36,791 --> 00:04:38,453
it doesn't stop the change.

130
00:04:38,616 --> 00:04:40,203
The change comes anyway,

131
00:04:40,203 --> 00:04:41,653
just without you steering it.

132
00:04:42,272 --> 00:04:44,997
Still, The Anchor is not a villain.

133
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:46,434
For our ancestors,

134
00:04:46,472 --> 00:04:48,259
it worked more like a seatbelt.

135
00:04:48,422 --> 00:04:50,472
Picture a small group on the savanna.

136
00:04:50,634 --> 00:04:52,384
They know one path to the water,

137
00:04:52,384 --> 00:04:54,934
and everyone who walked it came back alive.

138
00:04:55,097 --> 00:04:56,959
A new path might be shorter,

139
00:04:57,022 --> 00:04:58,584
but it might hide a lion.

140
00:04:58,747 --> 00:05:00,709
So the brain learned a simple rule:

141
00:05:00,897 --> 00:05:02,359
familiar means safe,

142
00:05:02,397 --> 00:05:06,160
because everything familiar is something you've already survived.

143
00:05:06,322 --> 00:05:07,410
On top of that,

144
00:05:07,522 --> 00:05:10,347
our ancestors lived with almost no margin,

145
00:05:10,559 --> 00:05:12,622
so losing what they had could mean starving,

146
00:05:12,772 --> 00:05:15,484
while gaining a little more was just a bonus.

147
00:05:15,647 --> 00:05:17,334
That's why, even today,

148
00:05:17,422 --> 00:05:20,222
losing something hurts much more than gaining the same

149
00:05:20,222 --> 00:05:21,072
thing feels good.

150
00:05:21,694 --> 00:05:23,044
Today, of course,

151
00:05:23,044 --> 00:05:24,944
a banking app won't eat anyone.

152
00:05:25,106 --> 00:05:26,781
But The Anchor doesn't know that,

153
00:05:26,969 --> 00:05:29,081
so it reacts to a new app as if

154
00:05:29,081 --> 00:05:30,731
it were a path full of lions.

155
00:05:30,894 --> 00:05:33,169
And here's what changes how you see Linda.

156
00:05:33,331 --> 00:05:35,306
This was never really about the app.

157
00:05:35,469 --> 00:05:37,856
For a woman who managed every coin on her

158
00:05:37,856 --> 00:05:39,069
own for forty years,

159
00:05:39,294 --> 00:05:41,131
the app means something much bigger:

160
00:05:41,381 --> 00:05:44,806
feeling clumsy, making a mistake she can't undo,

161
00:05:44,969 --> 00:05:47,594
and needing her daughter to explain things to her.

162
00:05:47,756 --> 00:05:50,206
So people don't really resist change.

163
00:05:50,369 --> 00:05:52,131
They resist losing something:

164
00:05:52,194 --> 00:05:54,319
their control, their confidence,

165
00:05:54,381 --> 00:05:56,331
their place as the one who knows.

166
00:05:56,494 --> 00:05:59,031
And the parents who cling the hardest are often

167
00:05:59,031 --> 00:06:01,606
the ones most afraid of feeling useless.

168
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:03,495
So one afternoon,

169
00:06:03,632 --> 00:06:05,820
Sarah invites her mom for a coffee.

170
00:06:05,982 --> 00:06:08,670
And instead of having the same argument one more

171
00:06:08,670 --> 00:06:11,295
time, she asks her just one question.

172
00:06:11,706 --> 00:06:15,777
"Mom, what scares you most about the app?" Linda

173
00:06:15,777 --> 00:06:17,215
starts to talk about hackers,

174
00:06:17,277 --> 00:06:18,615
about the tiny buttons,

175
00:06:18,677 --> 00:06:19,940
and then she stops,

176
00:06:20,002 --> 00:06:22,365
and the real answer comes out almost in a

177
00:06:22,365 --> 00:06:24,572
whisper. "Pressing the wrong thing,

178
00:06:24,585 --> 00:06:26,910
losing money, and you seeing that I can't do

179
00:06:26,910 --> 00:06:30,203
it alone." And Sarah takes her hand,

180
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:31,828
because for the first time,

181
00:06:31,978 --> 00:06:32,953
she gets it.

182
00:06:33,378 --> 00:06:34,415
"Then you'll do it,

183
00:06:34,415 --> 00:06:37,303
not me. I'll just sit next to you,

184
00:06:37,303 --> 00:06:41,235
and you keep your notebook." And that's the update.

185
00:06:41,398 --> 00:06:44,023
You can't pull up someone else's anchor by force,

186
00:06:44,023 --> 00:06:45,460
but you can help them lift it.

187
00:06:45,623 --> 00:06:46,948
Here are four ways.

188
00:06:47,110 --> 00:06:48,823
First, name the loss.

189
00:06:48,985 --> 00:06:50,898
Say out loud what they're giving up,

190
00:06:50,898 --> 00:06:54,223
because people stop defending what you've already acknowledged.

191
00:06:54,385 --> 00:06:56,373
Second, give them a role.

192
00:06:56,535 --> 00:06:58,798
Their experience isn't the obstacle.

193
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:01,285
Linda writes the steps in her own words,

194
00:07:01,423 --> 00:07:02,585
in her own notebook,

195
00:07:02,710 --> 00:07:04,285
and she holds the phone.

196
00:07:04,448 --> 00:07:05,960
Third, take small,

197
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:07,898
safe steps. This month,

198
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:09,385
only check the balance.

199
00:07:09,548 --> 00:07:11,348
Next month, pay one bill,

200
00:07:11,423 --> 00:07:13,385
with the bank line as a backup.

201
00:07:13,548 --> 00:07:16,235
And fourth, say clearly what stays.

202
00:07:16,398 --> 00:07:17,648
The notebook stays,

203
00:07:17,735 --> 00:07:19,223
the Sunday calls stay,

204
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:21,360
and she's still the one in charge of her

205
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:24,260
money. The anchor relaxes a lot when it knows

206
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:25,973
which ground is still solid.

207
00:07:26,135 --> 00:07:27,435
But one more thing.

208
00:07:27,598 --> 00:07:29,598
If a face popped into your head while you

209
00:07:29,598 --> 00:07:30,973
were watching, that's fine.

210
00:07:31,135 --> 00:07:33,210
Just check whether it's the one in your mirror.

211
00:07:33,373 --> 00:07:35,260
Is there a change you've been putting off for

212
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:37,260
months, waiting for the right moment?

213
00:07:37,894 --> 00:07:39,081
A month later,

214
00:07:39,081 --> 00:07:40,231
at the kitchen table,

215
00:07:40,456 --> 00:07:42,769
Linda pays her first bill in the app,

216
00:07:42,969 --> 00:07:44,881
with Sarah sitting next to her,

217
00:07:45,169 --> 00:07:46,356
hands in her lap,

218
00:07:46,606 --> 00:07:48,206
not touching anything.

219
00:07:48,369 --> 00:07:51,219
Linda stares at the screen for a long second.

220
00:07:51,652 --> 00:07:56,116
"That's it? That was easier than the line." Then

221
00:07:56,116 --> 00:07:57,991
she writes it down in her blue notebook,

222
00:07:58,016 --> 00:08:00,866
the date, the amount and a little check mark,

223
00:08:00,991 --> 00:08:03,166
like she has done for forty years.

224
00:08:03,328 --> 00:08:04,766
The Anchor is still there,

225
00:08:04,841 --> 00:08:06,453
but smaller and calmer,

226
00:08:06,478 --> 00:08:08,603
like an anchor resting in a harbor.

227
00:08:08,766 --> 00:08:10,103
And two weeks later,

228
00:08:10,103 --> 00:08:11,216
Sarah gets a call.

229
00:08:11,632 --> 00:08:13,757
"Your aunt Rosa doesn't know how to use the

230
00:08:13,757 --> 00:08:15,607
app. Don't worry,

231
00:08:15,607 --> 00:08:18,315
I'm teaching her." So,

232
00:08:18,315 --> 00:08:19,252
a quick recap.

233
00:08:19,415 --> 00:08:21,677
The Anchor makes people cling to the way things

234
00:08:21,677 --> 00:08:24,527
are. It exists because the known path was the

235
00:08:24,527 --> 00:08:27,065
one our ancestors had already survived.

236
00:08:27,227 --> 00:08:29,452
And you deal with it by naming the loss,

237
00:08:29,565 --> 00:08:30,965
giving people a role,

238
00:08:31,065 --> 00:08:33,915
taking small steps and saying what stays.

239
00:08:34,077 --> 00:08:36,127
Because people don't hate change.

240
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:37,540
They hate losing.

241
00:08:37,702 --> 00:08:39,152
Show them what they keep,

242
00:08:39,152 --> 00:08:41,065
and the anchor lets go on its own.

243
00:08:41,227 --> 00:08:42,727
So tell me in the comments:

244
00:08:42,977 --> 00:08:45,540
what's the one change someone in your family refuses

245
00:08:45,540 --> 00:08:47,440
to make? And subscribe,

246
00:08:47,602 --> 00:08:50,065
because next time we're meeting the voice of people

247
00:08:50,065 --> 00:08:52,265
who are sure they know exactly what you're thinking.
